Timeline
  Grepped every makefile and script for `emacs`: four targets in Makefile
  (forge-list, forge-pull, forge, forge-check), one dead call in
  gates/docs-lint.py (_unused_old_tangle_check, not reachable), and the
  `-Q -nw` validate lines in the standard-change.el header. No target
  combines `-nw` with `--batch`.
  Ran all four as they stood. All four exited 0. forge-check and
  forge-list did what they claim. forge-pull worked here only because the
  forge answered in under twelve seconds.
  Probed batch exit semantics: an error at top level exits 255, but an
  error raised inside a timer prints "Error running timer" and exits 0.
  That is the shape of an async forge pull's failure path.
  Reproduced the silent no-op: with the wait cut to 0.3s the pull prints
  "Pulling..." with no "...done", stores nothing, and exits 0.
  Fixed, then negative-tested each direction (see the commit message).

Reproduction
  $ gmake forge-pull          # 4.0s, "forge: pulled", exit 0
  $ emacs --batch -l standard-change.el \
      --eval '(setq forge-database-file "/tmp/copy.sqlite" \
                    standard-change-forge-pull-timeout 0.3)' \
      -f standard-change-forge-pull
    forge: pull unfinished after 0.3s -- the database was NOT refreshed
    exit 1                                      # was: silent, exit 0
  $ emacs --batch -l standard-change.el --eval '(setq auth-sources nil)' \
      -f standard-change-forge-check
    forge-check: FAIL -- api token
    exit 1                                      # was: "MISSING", exit 0
  $ emacs --batch -l standard-change.el -f standard-change-status
    prints the queue                            # was: no output, exit 0

